// wdoc section: Diagrams.

// Classes used by the "Styling shapes" example below — declared at the top
// level so they apply across the site; shapes opt in via their `class` list.
// Hyphenated names must be quoted (a bare identifier can't contain `-`).
class "dgm-box" {
  fill         = "#5e81ac"
  stroke       = "#2e3440"
  stroke_width = "2"
  dark  { fill = "#88c0d0"  stroke = "#eceff4" }
  light { fill = "#5e81ac"  stroke = "#2e3440" }
}
class "dgm-ghost" {
  fill    = "#a3be8c"
  opacity = "0.5"
}

page wdoc_diagrams { sites = [:reference]
  h1 "Diagrams"

  p "Diagram blocks are wdoc's way of drawing diagrams and charts on pages. A diagram is made of shapes that break down into primitive shapes and render as SVG, which lets the same source target different backends."

  h2 "The diagram block"
  
  p "Diagrams have the following properties that can be set."

  type_table { type = Diagram }

  h2 "Pan & zoom"

  p "Set `pan_zoom = true` to wrap the diagram in an interactive viewport. Wheel zooms toward the cursor, drag pans, the corner buttons zoom about centre / reset. Zoom is clamped to `[zoom_min, zoom_max]`; pan allows half-a-viewport of overscroll past each content edge plus the author's `pan_margin`. Try it on the diagram below:"

  diagram {
    width = 320  height = 160  pan_zoom = true
    zoom_min = 0.5  zoom_max = 4.0
    rect { id = pz_a  x = 20.0   y = 30.0  width = 80.0  height = 50.0  fill = "#88c0d0" }
    rect { id = pz_b  x = 210.0  y = 90.0  width = 80.0  height = 50.0  fill = "#a3be8c" }
    pz_a -> pz_b
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
diagram {
  width = 320  height = 160  pan_zoom = true
  zoom_min = 0.5  zoom_max = 4.0
  rect { id = a  x = 20.0   y = 30.0  width = 80.0  height = 50.0  fill = "#88c0d0" }
  rect { id = b  x = 210.0  y = 90.0  width = 80.0  height = 50.0  fill = "#a3be8c" }
  a -> b
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "Styling shapes with classes"

  p "Every shape takes a `class` list, just like text. A `class` emits SVG paint properties — `fill`, `stroke`, `stroke_width`, `stroke_linecap`, `stroke_linejoin`, `opacity` — with `dark { }` / `light { }` overrides, so you style shapes the same way you style prose. Prefer a class over an inline `fill = …` so a shape follows the site theme and the reader's light/dark mode."

  diagram { width = 320  height = 120
    rect { x = 20.0   y = 25.0  width = 110.0  height = 70.0  class = ["dgm-box"] }
    rect { x = 160.0  y = 25.0  width = 110.0  height = 70.0  class = ["dgm-ghost"] }
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
// Declared at the top level (apply site-wide). Quote hyphenated names.
class "dgm-box" {
  fill         = "#5e81ac"
  stroke       = "#2e3440"
  stroke_width = "2"
  dark  { fill = "#88c0d0"  stroke = "#eceff4" }
  light { fill = "#5e81ac"  stroke = "#2e3440" }
}
class "dgm-ghost" {
  fill    = "#a3be8c"
  opacity = "0.5"
}

diagram { width = 320  height = 120
  rect { x = 20.0   y = 25.0  width = 110.0  height = 70.0  class = ["dgm-box"] }
  rect { x = 160.0  y = 25.0  width = 110.0  height = 70.0  class = ["dgm-ghost"] }
}
WCL
  }

  p "The SVG paint fields a `class` understands on a shape:"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Effect" |
      | "`fill`"                  | "Interior colour." |
      | "`stroke`"                | "Outline colour." |
      | "`stroke_width`"          | "Outline thickness." |
      | "`stroke_linecap` / `stroke_linejoin`" | "Line end / corner style." |
      | "`opacity`"               | "Overall transparency (`0`–`1`)." |
      | "`dark { }` / `light { }`" | "Per-colour-scheme overrides." |
  }

  p "Built-in shape classes work the same way — redeclare a quoted name to recolour it everywhere. For example, the chart palette (`wdoc-series-1`..`wdoc-series-8`) or the timeline parts:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
class "wdoc-series-1" {
  fill   = "#bf616a"
  stroke = "#bf616a"
  dark  { fill = "#d08770" }
}
WCL
  }

  p "See [Styling](wdoc_styling) for the full class + theme reference."

  p "Next: [Primitive Shapes](wdoc_primitives)."
}
